3. Master Equation and Theoretical Core

3.1 The CMB Master Action

[ \mathcal{S}{\text{CMB}}[\mathbb{E}, \mathbb{W}] = \int{\mathcal{M}} d^4x ,\sqrt{-\det \mathbb{W}}, \Big( \sum_i \alpha_i \mathbb{B}_i(\mathbb{E}, \mathbb{W}) \Big) ]

(\mathbb{W}): geometry/warp tensor

(\mathbb{E}): field‑organism set

(\mathbb{B}_i): encrypted design blocks

3.2 Variational Principle

(\delta \mathcal{S}_{\text{CMB}} = 0) as the single root equation.

Known regimes (GR, MHD, etc.) as projections/limits (described conceptually, not explicitly).

3.3 Projection Profiles

Concept of ProjectionProfile (e.g. relativistic_plasma, weak_warp, axial).

How profiles select limits and symmetries without naming the underlying physics.

4. Encrypted Block Architecture

4.1 Block Tokens and Registry

BlockToken (e.g. WARP_CORE, GRAV_SHELL, PLASMA_SKIN, ORGANISM_HEART).

BlockRegistry for token → implementation lookup.

Zero semantic leakage: registry stores no physics names.

4.2 Block Structure

Each block defines:

Required tensors (anonymous shapes)

Kernel specs (opcodes, inputs, outputs, params)

Contribution to the effective action

4.3 Context and Projection

context object: grid size, time step, projection profile.

Blocks adapt their kernels to the active profile.

5. DSL and Scenario Configuration

5.1 .cmb Scenario Files

Example:

engine: CMB_REBIRTH
equation_root: S_CMB

blocks:
  - WARP_CORE
  - GRAV_SHELL
  - PLASMA_SKIN
  - ORGANISM_HEART

projections:
  regime: relativistic_plasma
  symmetry: axial
  limit: weak_warp

constants:
  G:   G_CMB
  c:   c_REBIRTH
  L:   L_IND

5.2 Parsing Pipeline

Scenario → Engine + ActionRoot

Tokens → Blocks via BlockRegistry

Projections → ProjectionProfile

5.3 Zero‑Leakage at Rest

Files contain only tokens, branded constants, and profiles.

No explicit equation names or physical labels.

6. Numerical Integrator Layer

6.1 Kernel Graph Representation

KernelSpec:

id, op_code, inputs, outputs, params

Blocks emit kernel DAGs.

6.2 Tensor Handles

Anonymous TensorHandle objects: T0, T1, …

Shapes and dtypes only; no semantic names.

6.3 Execution Backend

Generic opcodes: MATMUL, STENCIL_3D, AXPY, REDUCE, etc.

Backend dispatch loop over kernel graph.

Zero semantic leakage at runtime: backend sees only opcodes + arrays.

6.4 Stability and Performance Considerations

Time stepping strategies (high‑level).

Parallelization and GPU suitability (conceptual).

7. Rendering and Visual Grammar

7.1 Tensor Signature Detection

Shape‑based classification: scalar, vector, rank‑2 tensor, particles.

Mapping signatures → visual grammars (volume, streamlines, glyphs, point clouds).

7.2 Shader Interface

Uniforms: u_tensor, u_colorPrimary, u_colorSecondary, u_intensity, u_time, u_basis.

Anonymous tensor sampling and palette‑driven coloring.

7.3 Mock Fragment Shader Template

Tensor sampling function.

Palette application (applyCMBPalette).

Time‑based pulse/shimmer for CMB signature.

7.4 Color Palette Configuration

Tokens like AMBER_CORE_DECAY, BIOLUMINESCENT_CYAN.

Mapping theme configs → shader uniforms.

Visual identity as the only semantic layer.

8. Security and Zero‑Leakage Properties

8.1 At Rest

.cmb and theme files contain only tokens and numeric parameters.

No explicit equations or model names.

8.2 At Runtime

Integrator operates on anonymous tensors and generic kernels.

Memory dumps reveal only arrays and opcodes.

8.3 At Interface

Only visual grammar and palette tokens expose identity.

Physics meaning is recoverable only via private internal mappings.

